{
    "title": "Airworthiness Directives; Pilatus Aircraft Ltd. Airplanes",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 25, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-25/pdf/2025-21090.pdf",
    "summary": "The U.S. aviation regulator has issued several new safety rules aimed at tightening maintenance practices and updating procedures for certain jets and helicopters. The changes focus on preventing navigation errors during approaches, improving detection of loosening hardware on tail rotors, and speeding up how quickly airlines update their maintenance programs.\n\nFor business jet operators, a new rule affects the Pilatus PC\u201124 after a report of the flight director giving inaccurate guidance on approach in some software versions. Pilots must add a temporary update to the flight manual\u2019s abnormal procedures and fly accordingly. The agency calls this an interim step while the manufacturer continues its investigation, and it notes the change is quick and low cost to implement. The goal is to reduce pilot workload and keep approach paths stable and predictable.\n\nHelicopter operators flying the Airbus EC120B will see a superseding rule that keeps the existing inspections of the tail rotor in place and adds a required modification: red paint marks on key hardware to make any loss of tightening immediately visible. Once this is done, crews will inspect the paint line regularly for alignment. This approach is intended to catch early signs of loosening before they become a safety risk, and it replaces some of the repeated, more intrusive checks over time.\n\nSeparately, the regulator is also adopting updates tied to European safety directives for certain large jets, with a notable twist: U.S. operators must revise their maintenance or inspection programs within 90 days rather than the longer window used in Europe. After these updates are made, no alternative inspection actions or intervals are allowed unless specifically approved.\n\nWho benefits? Passengers, pilots, and maintenance teams all gain from clearer procedures, earlier detection of problems, and closer alignment between U.S. and European safety expectations. The Pilatus change in particular provides immediate guidance to crews, while the helicopter update gives maintainers a simple visual cue that can prevent bigger issues. Regulators also benefit from more consistent oversight and faster adoption of known safety improvements.\n\nWho may feel the pinch? Operators will face short compliance timelines, added paperwork, and possible schedule disruptions while they update manuals, log records, and perform the new checks and markings. Smaller operators with lean staffing might find the 90\u2011day maintenance program revisions especially tight. Helicopter fleets will need to plan for the modification and recurring inspections, and flight departments may need to brief crews and adjust training and standard procedures.\n\nOverall, these steps are measured, relatively low cost, and focused on practical risk reduction, with the possibility of further changes as manufacturers complete their investigations.",
